.TH snip 1 "" "snip 0.6.3" "Snip Manual"
.SH "NAME"
snip \- filesystem\-native snippet library
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBsnip\fR [\fB\-\-library\fR] [\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-simplified\-ui\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.RS 4
.nf
snip [OPTIONS] <SELECTOR>
.fi
.RE
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
snip stores code snippets as ordinary files in a .sniplib directory. It provides a scriptable command\-line interface and an optional interactive terminal browser while keeping the filesystem as the source of truth.
.PP
Library resolution follows this order: \-\-library, SNIP_LIBRARY, the nearest ancestor containing snip.toml, then default_library from the user configuration. Commands that need a library fail instead of silently creating one.
.PP
For quick human lookup, snip <selector> is equivalent to snip preview <selector>, including headers. Options belong on the explicit preview command, and a title that matches a subcommand name must be opened with snip preview because command names take precedence.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-library\fR \fI<LIBRARY>\fR
Library root. Falls back to SNIP_LIBRARY, ancestor discovery, then config
.TP
\fB\-\-output\fR \fI<OUTPUT>\fR
Structured output mode for commands that return records
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
human
.IP \(bu 2
json
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<COLOR>\fR
Color policy for terminal preview
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
always
.IP \(bu 2
never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-simplified\-ui\fR[=\fI<BOOL>\fR]
Use square TUI bars without Powerline font glyphs for this run
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
